@* Nothing at this address (TFND-128).

   A CATCH-ALL page rather than the Router's <NotFound> fragment, because the
   two cover different halves of the same problem. <NotFound> fires only for
   client-side navigation inside a live circuit; a browser requesting an unknown
   URL from cold hits the server, and with MapFallbackToFile retired there was
   no endpoint to answer it — the reader got a bare ASP.NET 404 with no shell,
   no navigation and nothing to click.

   Catch-all routes lose to literal and constrained ones, so this cannot shadow
   a real screen. *@
@page "/{*Path}"
@attribute [Authorize]
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<PageTitle>Not found — tamp.findings</PageTitle>

<div class="stack">
    <h1>Nothing at this address</h1>
    <Card>
        <p><code class="mono">@CurrentPath</code> does not match any screen.</p>
        <p class="field__help">
            If this was a bookmark, the client or project in the URL may have been renamed — the name
            is part of the address, so a rename moves the page.
        </p>
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="@Routes.Portfolio">Back to the portfolio</a>
    </Card>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>Whatever was asked for. Captured so the page can name it.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Path { get; set; }

    private string CurrentPath => "/" + (Path ?? Nav.ToBaseRelativePath(Nav.Uri));
}
